in this video we're going to go back to  school and we're going to cover some of  the basic terminology of email some of  this may be very familiar to you some of  it will be new let's get started and  talk about the most basic element of  email and that's the list whenever you  hear email marketers talk they're going  to be referring to the list and simply  the list is the list of email addresses  that you have this list can be broken up  in many ways but ultimately the list is  your customers your prospects anyone who  has said they want to receive an email  from you but ultimately the list is the  group of people you are going to email  now you can grow your list by collecting  email addresses the majority of  companies collect the email address from  a transaction on the website they can  come from many other methods but just  gathering those names from your website  from people that say they want to hear  more from you or they want to receive  information is the primary way of  building that now the email server is  very much like the post office of email  it routes your email from your computer  to the intended destination and so it's  essentially the post office with the  mailman taking your email routing it  through the server to go to the person  that you are sending that email and so  it can be your business or internal  server that you have inside your company  it can be a third party service such as  a GoDaddy or Rackspace or you can be  using a third party email service such  as Constant Contact  MailChimp some of those that will take  care of your email marketing list and  will be that third party go-between for  sending out a mass amount of email to  different people and that third  party handles all of the legalities and  the sending options for that so an ESP  is an email service provider they are  the ones that can handle and maintain  your list as well as enable you to build  an email through their service and send  it to those people and the email service  provider will enable better management  of your list now one of the  methodologies of sending email that is  called a batch and blasts is taking the  same email message and sending it to  everybody on the list typically this is  done with a newsletter or a company  update and what it does is it assumes  that everyone is interested in this  message so that's why we call it the  batching blast we're taking all the  names that we have and we're sending  them the exact same email now sometimes  for best practices we tend to back off  of this approach because we've found  that targeted emails tend to work better  but batch and blast it's just sort of an  offhanded way of saying we're sending  the same newsletter or the same  communication to everybody we have on  that list now how you build your list is  very important how people have let you  know that they want to receive an email  message is critical and so an opt-in is  when someone allows you and gives you  permission to send them an email an  opt-in is one of those very critical  factors that helps establish your  company as following best practices in  email marketing and opt-out is where you  allow people to say I don't want to  receive email from you and so it  requires action on the part of the  recipient to say I no longer want to  receive email from you in a lot of laws  require opt-outs to be part of mass  mailings so that a recipient can opt out  of uture mailings at any time an opt-out  can also be called an unsubscribe where  they can go in and say  I no longer want to receive email  messages from you as a company or I just  want to unsubscribe from this list and  so giving people the ability to manage  their own email contact settings is  something that allows you to comply to  spam laws but also gives your customer  additional ability to manage their  contact preferences now I've mentioned  the word spam numerous times but chances  are you know what that is spam is  anything that you receive that's  unsolicited or unsubscribed if you've  never subscribed to receive email from a  specific vendor or a person or an  organization and it shows up in your  email box anyway that's considered spam  there are incredibly powerful spam  filters on email servers everywhere and  so you need to make sure that you are  abiding by best practices and spam laws  in order for your email not to be  considered spam in talking about spam  what differentiates different types of  messages is the understanding of what  makes a transactional email if someone  is purchasing from you if they've  requested information if they're looking  for a download that is a transaction  they have received value for giving you  their email address and that determines  moving forward if they are receiving  spam or if they have opted in to receive  email messages from you now a blacklist  is something that you do not want your  company to be on a blacklist is set  aside by email servers as a gateway to  preventing people who have repeatedly  abused email by sending spam by setting  unsolicited email to other people and so  the blacklist limits the ability of  those organizations or those mail  sources to send unsolicited email the  open rate is how many people opened your  email to view it the first measurement  that people typically look at we  comes to email marketing is the open  rate and it's also the first trap that  they can fall into they tend to view  open rates as the primary indicator of  success of an email marketing campaign  but unfortunately open rate doesn't tell  the full story and it's more of a fuzzy  number because not all email clients  report the open rate the same  it's very undependable and so really  just use the open rate as a guide rather  than focusing on it as a measure of  success a click-through is much more of  a hard measurement of how people  interacted with your email so whereas we  can look at the number of opens and get  an idea of how many people looked the  click tells us how many people took an  action to click through the email to the  landing page that we have set up for  that destination and so it's much more  of a hard number to look at success of  an email campaign now the email client  is whatever your target recipient uses  to view email it could be Outlook  it could be Mac mail it's whatever they  use in order to open the email that's  the email client now there's also it's  called free mail and that's any free  email service such as Gmail or yahoo  mail or hotmail  their web-based email accounts which act  a little bit differently than corporate  email accounts or other types of email  accounts and so that's going to come in  when we look at deliverability and how  your message appears to the recipient  you
 

